# Locus Server — Secrets and transient files

# Plain-text secrets file (NEVER commit — encrypt before committing).
# The ENCRYPTED form, secrets.env.age, IS committed on purpose: it is encrypted
# to a passphrase, and a clone plus that passphrase is the whole recovery story.
.secrets.env

# Passphrase material for secrets.env.age. These patterns exist because the
# passphrase is the ONLY thing protecting the committed ciphertext, so a
# plaintext copy of it in the working tree is the highest-value file in the
# repo — higher than any single credential inside it.
#
# The patterns carry NO `./` prefix, for the reason recorded in the root
# .gitignore: gitignore matches relative to the .gitignore's own directory, so a
# `./name` pattern matched nothing and `git add -A` staged a live credential
# (caught 2026-10-02, vpspassword.txt).
#
# Covers the documented env-var convention (LOCUS_SECRETS_PASSPHRASE put in a
# file for an agent or a script to read) and the obvious ad-hoc names.
encryption-password*
secrets-passphrase*
secrets-pass*
.passphrase
passphrase.txt

# NOTE: the age KEYPAIR files are gone. `age-key.txt` was gitignored here until
# 2026-10-16; the secrets file is now encrypted to a passphrase, so no key file
# exists to ignore. If you still have an old checkout with one, delete it — it
# can no longer decrypt anything in this repo.

# VPS-specific runtime files (created by setup.sh on the VPS)
*.tmp
/tmp/myvpn-*

# Backup copies written by scripts/secrets-encrypt.sh before it overwrites the
# ciphertext. An old ciphertext is not secret, but it is confusing: it no longer
# matches what the deploy scripts expect, and a future reader will not know
# which passphrase opens which copy.
secrets.env.age.bak.*
